   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Michael,<div>    So I&#39;ve had a bit more time to play around with the function on PC and Mac and found that I receive the same error if the site is anywhere east of the -100.00 longitude line for the US. West of the line it works well on Mac and with the modified ncdf4 package for PC (coast and inland). I&#39;ve tried random locations using both decimal degrees and quotations around cities but run into the same issue for anything east of that line. The error appears to happen right after attempting to extract the &#39;tmin&#39; weather input. </div><div><br></div><div>While searching for the error I saw something about the coordinate format (double vs. index) but I am pretty unfamiliar with the specifics/conversions. </div><div><br></div><div>Thanks for all your help,</div><div>Adam</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "NaIxCgvBAQAJ",
   "from": "Michael Kearney",
   "author": "e46cb5dd353b7dfb",
   "date": "2018-08-21T11:06:45Z",
   "subject": "Re: micro_USA() function error",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi Adam,</div><div><br></div><div>Very sorry this has taken so long. I&#39;ve fixed the problem - it seems that there was a change in the netCDF file setup where the order of latitude and longitude was switched. It should work now. Note, however, that with R v3.5 the ncdf4 version from <a href=\"https://github.com/pmjherman/r-ncdf4-build-opendap-windows\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/pmjherman/r-ncdf4-build-opendap-windows&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793240439265000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2TmmbjQe3ZhrLcOmRt0anw\">https://github.com/pmjherman/r-ncdf4-build-opendap-windows</a> doesn&#39;t seem to work any longer :(.<br></div><div><br></div><div>Thanks for alerting me to the problem!</div><div><br></div><div>All the best,<br>Mike</div><div><br></div><div>p.s. it&#39;s easy to download the grids if you have the local storage space, and then your queries to the data will be faster: <a href=\"http://www.climatologylab.org/gridmet.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.climatologylab.org/gridmet.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793240439265000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2pRQVG72k2zig_iGE0YPEZ\">http://www.climatologylab.org/gridmet.html</a><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
